 at first publication, use the table value for all industries month-to-month percent change. Ignore later revisions.\",\n./site/src/data/ledger-targets.generated.ts:6945:    note: \"Target registration generated from the recorded thesis.analyst run for Canada GDP by industry, May 2026.\",\n./docs/thesis-vision.md:48:Good targets include labor-market releases, CPI and PCE releases, GDP and\n./site/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:1636:          text: \"Forecast: Canada real GDP by industry monthly growth for April 2026\",\n./site/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:1640:          text: \"Resolver is Statistics Canada's first-print seasonally adjusted monthly percent change for real GDP by industry at basic prices for April 2026, not a later revised table value.\",\n./site/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:1645:          call: \"Checked Statistics Canada major economic release calendar for the April 2026 GDP by industry release date.\",\n./site/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:1652:          call: \"Read the Statistics Canada March 2026 GDP by industry Daily release for latest official monthly growth and April advance estimate.\",\n./site/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:1654:            \"March 2026 real GDP by industry was -0.1%; goods-producing industries were -0.8%; services-producing industries were +0.1%; the advance estimate for April 2026 was +0.4%.\",\n./site/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:1659:          call: \"Read prior Statistics Canada GDP by industry releases for recent base rates and advance-estimate accuracy.\",\n./site/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:1661:            \"January 2026 GDP by industry was +0.1% and its February advance was +0.2%; February 2026 was +0.2% and its March advance was essentially 0.0%; March later printed -0.1%.\",\n./site/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:1676:          text: \"Counter-consideration: the advance estimate can miss by a tenth or more, and April's petroleum-price shock boosts nominal indicators more than real GDP; missing retail and some service indicators could pull the final first print below the advance.\",\n./site/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:2011:                \"resolutionSourceUrl points to the May 29, 2026 March GDP release, not the June 30, 2026 April first-print release or a durable table endpoint.\",\n./site/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:2092:          text: \"Forecast for Canada April 2026 real GDP by industry monthly growth\",\n./site/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:2096:          text: \"Resolver is the first Statistics Canada print for seasonally adjusted real GDP by industry, all industries, month-to-month percent change for April 2026. The target is the June 30, 2026 Daily/table release, not later revised history.\",\n./site/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:2103:            \"The schedule lists Gross domestic product by industry for April 2026 with release date June 30, 2026; adjacent GDP by industry releases are May 29, 2026 for March 2026 and July 31, 2026 for May 2026.\",\n./site/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:2110:            \"The March 2026 release reported real GDP by industry at -0.1% month to month, after February +0.2%, and gave an April 2026 advance estimate of +0.4%; goods-producing industries were -0.8% in March and services-producing industries were +0.1%.\",\n./site/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:2132:          text: \"Inside-view decomposition: level effects are modest because Q1 GDP by industry was only +0.1 for the quarter. Momentum is mixed after March weakness, but the named April advance components point to rebounds in mining, quarrying, oil and gas extraction, manufacturing, and transportation and warehousing. One-off factors include March maintenance/weather disruptions in energy and auto-related volatility that can reverse. Policy/trade mechanisms remain a drag risk through tariffs and manufacturing/export uncertainty.\",\n./site/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:4798:        \"Q1 GDP fell 0.2%, raising mild upside risk\",\n./site/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:4923:            \"Fetched official listing values: GDP Q1 2026 down 0.2% in euro area, employment Q1 2026 up 0.1%, May 2026 euro area inflation flash 3.2%, April 2026 retail trade down 0.4%.\",\n./site/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:4942:          text: \"Counter-consideration: Q1 GDP contraction of 0.2%, weaker retail trade, and higher May inflation at 3.2% could weaken demand and push unemployment up. These forces justify keeping upside mass at 6.4-6.5 rather than making the interval too narrow.\",\n./site/src/data/forecast-examples/global-near-term.ts:34:    chainableQuestions: [\"next release\", \"+3 months\", \"GDP contribution\"],\n./site/src/data/forecast-examples/global-near-term.ts:99:      \"Wholesale trade is an early supply-chain and business-demand signal that feeds Canadian GDP by industry and inventory-cycle forecasts.\",\n./site/src/data/forecast-examples/global-near-term.ts:284:    chainableQuestions: [\"next release\", \"+3 months\", \"GDP contribution\"],\n./site/src/data/forecast-examples/uk.ts:12:    \"ONS GDP monthly estimate\",\n./site/src/data/forecast-examples/uk.ts:25:    title: \"UK monthly GDP growth\",\n./site/src/data/forecast-examples/uk.ts:26:    measure: \"Monthly real GDP growth, seasonally adjusted\",\n./site/src/data/forecast-examples/uk.ts:28:    source: \"Office for National Statistics, GDP monthly estimate\",\n./site/src/data/forecast-examples/uk.ts:36:      \"Monthly GDP is the fastest official read on UK growth and feeds directly into fiscal, labour-market, and monetary-policy predictions.\",\n./site/src/data/forecast-examples/uk.ts:50:      call: 'ons.lookup({ release: \"GDP monthly estimate\", series: \"monthly_real_gdp_growth\", months: [\"2026-01\", \"2026-03\"] })',\n./site/src/data/forecast-e