th because quarterly estimated taxes arrive ~June 15. Excluding the 2020-2021 pandemic, the last six Junes (2018,2019,2022,2023,2024,2025) ranged from a $228B deficit to a $27B surplus, mean ~ -$73B deficit, median ~ -$71B deficit (sign converted: deficit positive ~ +71 to +73).', null, 'public_only'),\\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-backfill.sql:242749:  ('reasoning.run.us-mts-deficit-june-2026.2026-06-12T18-59-50Z.1', 'run.us-mts-deficit-june-2026.2026-06-12T18-59-50Z', 1, 'assistant_message', 'FY2026 is running better than FY2025: cumulative deficit ~9% ($118B) lower through May, receipts +$174B YoY (CRFB). Since Jun2025 was already a +$27B surplus, a similarly strong receipts month in Jun2026 argues for an outcome near balance to a small deficit, modestly worse than 2025 only if outlays (interest/entitlements, +$56B YoY) dominate.', null, 'public_only'),\\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-backfill.sql:242750:  ('reasoning.run.us-mts-deficit-june-2026.2026-06-12T18-59-50Z.2', 'run.us-mts-deficit-june-2026.2026-06-12T18-59-50Z', 2, 'assistant_message', 'Base rate for the deficit-positive figure: recent Junes cluster ~ -$30B (surplus) to +$230B (deficit), with most mass in the +$0B to +$90B deficit range once pandemic years are dropped. The unconditional centre is a small-to-moderate deficit; a surplus (like 2025) is plausible but not modal across the full sample.', null, 'public_only'),\\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-backfill.sql:242751:  ('reasoning.run.us-mts-deficit-june-2026.2026-06-12T18-59-50Z.3', 'run.us-mts-deficit-june-2026.2026-06-12T18-59-50Z', 3, 'assistant_message', 'June-to-June dispersion (ex-pandemic) is large: the six clean Junes have a population stdev of roughly $80-90B around their mean. Calendar shifts (benefit-payment timing when month-ends fall on weekends) routinely move a single month by $50-100B, so the 80% band must be wide (~+/-$110-130B).', null, 'public_only'),\\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-backfill.sql:242752:  ('reasoning.run.us-mts-deficit-june-2026.2026-06-12T18-59-50Z.4', 'run.us-mts-deficit-june-2026.2026-06-12T18-59-50Z', 4, 'assistant_message', 'Outside [-90, +170] if: (a) a benefit-payment calendar shift or weak quarterly receipts produces a large deficit >$170B (cf. Jun2023 +$228B); or (b) an exceptionally strong receipts month plus tariff revenue yields a surplus larger than ~$90B. The Middle East shock is roughly fiscal-neutral month-to-month here.', null, 'public_only'),\\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-backfill.sql:242753:  ('reasoning.run.us-mts-deficit-june-2026.2026-06-12T18-59-50Z.5', 'run.us-mts-deficit-june-2026.2026-06-12T18-59-50Z', 5, 'assistant_message', 'Anchor on the ex-pandemic June mean (~+$73B deficit) but shade toward near-balance given FY2026''s stronger receipts and the 2025 surplus precedent -> point +$25B deficit. 80% CI = +25 +/- ~$120-130B, slightly asymmetric (surpluses bounded near -$90B, deficit tail to +$170B) -> [-90, 170].', null, 'public_only'),\\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-backfill.sql:242754:  ('reasoning.run.us-mts-deficit-june-2026.2026-06-12T18-59-50Z.6', 'run.us-mts-deficit-june-2026.2026-06-12T18-59-50Z', 6, 'assistant_message', 'Published forecast: point 25, 80% interval [-90, 170].', null, 'public_only'),\\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-backfill.sql:242755:  ('reasoning.run.us-mts-deficit-june-2026.2026-06-12T18-59-50Z.time-series-prior.0', 'run.us-mts-deficit-june-2026.2026-06-12T18-59-50Z.time-series-prior', 0, 'assistant_message', 'Time-series prior', null, 'public_only'),\\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-backfill.sql:242756:  ('reasoning.run.us-mts-deficit-june-2026.2026-06-12T18-59-50Z.time-series-prior.1', 'run.us-mts-deficit-june-2026.2026-06-12T18-59-50Z.time-series-prior', 1, 'assistant_message', 'Prior point = latest observed value = 293; 80% interval = [106, 479].', null, 'public_only'),\\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-backfill.sql:242757:  ('reasoning.