of the Fiscal Service first report as the federal government's current-month deficit or surplus for July 2026 in Monthly Treasury Statement Table 1, in nominal USD billions, with deficits positive and surpluses negative?\\\",\\\"unit\\\":\\\"usd_billions\\\",\\\"pointEstimate\\\":310,\\\"ciLow\\\":170,\\\"ciHigh\\\":470,\\\"confidence\\\":0.8,\\\"resolutionDate\\\":\\\"2026-08-12\\\",\\\"resolutionSource\\\":\\\"Official agency release\\\",\\\"resolutionSourceUrl\\\":\\\"https://fiscaldata.treasury.gov/datasets/monthly-treasury-statement/summary-of-receipts-outlays-and-the-deficit-surplus-of-the-u-s-government\\\",\\\"resolutionRule\\\":\\\"Resolve to the July 2026 current-month Total Surplus (+) or Deficit (-) in the first official U.S. Treasury Bureau of the Fiscal Service Monthly Treasury Statement Table 1 print, converted from millions to billions of nominal dollars and sign-flipped so deficits are positive and surpluses are negative. Use the first published MTS table; later revisions or FiscalData refreshes do not change the resolved value unless Treasury replaces the first table on the same publication date.\\\",\\\"dataPointId\\\":\\\"treasury.mts.monthly_deficit.july_2026.first_print\\\",\\\"historicalContext\\\":[{\\\"label\\\":\\\"July 2022 deficit, MTS Table 1\\\",\\\"value\\\":211.052},{\\\"label\\\":\\\"July 2023 deficit, MTS Table 1\\\",\\\"value\\\":220.782},{\\\"label\\\":\\\"July 2024 deficit, MTS Table 1\\\",\\\"value\\\":243.741},{\\\"label\\\":\\\"FY2026 Jan-Apr history in catalog context\\\",\\\"value\\\":953.6},{\\\"label\\\":\\\"May 2025 deficit, MTS Table 1 catalog context\\\",\\\"value\\\":315.7}],\\\"drivers\\\":[\\\"July usually has no major individual or corporate tax due date\\\",\\\"benefit and Medicare outlays continue to trend upward in nominal dollars\\\",\\\"August 1, 2026 falls on a Saturday, likely pulling some August payments into July\\\",\\\"elevated customs receipts reduce the deficit relative to gross-outlay pressure\\\",\\\"refund and tariff-refund timing can move the monthly balance materially\\\"],\\\"sourceContext\\\":[\\\"https://fiscaldata.treasury.gov/release-calendar/\\\",\\\"https://fiscaldata.treasury.gov/datasets/monthly-treasury-statement/summary-of-receipts-outlays-and-the-deficit-surplus-of-the-u-s-government\\\",\\\"https://fiscal.treasury.gov/files/reports-statements/mts/mts0724.pdf\\\",\\\"https://fiscal.treasury.gov/files/reports-statements/mts/mts0723.pdf\\\",\\\"https://fiscal.treasury.gov/resources/reports-statements\\\"],\\\"runAt\\\":\\\"2026-07-04T19:17:15Z\\\",\\\"reasoning\\\":[{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"heading\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Forecast for July 2026 Monthly Treasury Statement deficit\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"The target is the first official Treasury MTS Table 1 current-month deficit/surplus for July 2026, in USD billions, with deficits reported as positive values. I treat FiscalData/MTS as the resolver and do not use later revisions.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"official.lookup\\\",\\\"call\\\":\\\"Opened the Fiscal Data release-calendar surface and the official MTS publication text to verify the release vehicle and timing rule.\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"Fetched official calendar surface for 2026 and the MTS statement that releases are normally on the 8th workday after the reporting month; for July 2026 the 8th workday after July 31 is 2026-08-12.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"official.lookup\\\",\\\"call\\\":\\\"Opened the July 2024 Monthly Treasury Statement PDF and read Table 1 and the July highlight note.\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"Fetched July 2024 Table 1: receipts 330.377 billion, outlays 574.119 billion, deficit 243.741 billion; the MTS note says July had been a deficit month 68 times out of 70 fiscal years because there are usually no major corporate or individual tax due dates.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"official.lookup\\\",\\\"call\\\":\\\"Opened the July 2023 Monthly Treasury Statement PDF and read Table 1 plus the payment-timing highlight.\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"Fetched July 2023 Table 1: receipts 276.161 billion, outlays 496.943 billion, deficit 220.782 billion; the highlight states some July 1, 2023 payments accelerated into June because July 1 was a non-business day.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"official.lookup\\\",\\\"call\\\":\\\"Read the prior-year section in the official July 2023 MTS Table 1 for the July 2022 reference point.\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"Fetched July 2022 Table 1 from the official July 2023 MTS comparison: receipts 269.331 billion, outlays 480.383 billion, deficit 211.052 billion.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"tool\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"repo.lookup\\\",\\\"call\\\":\\\"Inspected public Thesis catalog context for the same Treasury MTS monthly-deficit series without using catalog point forecasts as evidence.\\\",\\\"result\\\":\\\"Fetched official-source-derived series context: FY2026 Jan-Apr deficit 953.6 billion, April 2026 surplus 215.0 billion, May 2025 deficit 315.7 billion, and MTS release rule 8th business day after month end.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Base-rate/reference-class anchor: the clean outside view is recent official July MTS first prints. July 2022-2024 deficits were 211.052, 220.782, and 243.741 billion, averaging about 225.2 billion, with a rising but still fairly tight pre-2026 range.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Level, momentum, and mechanism: nominal outlays should be above the 2022-2024 level because Social Security, Medicare, health, and net interest are higher. July 2026 also has an important calendar feature: August 1 falls on a Saturday, so payments normally due August 1 can be made on July 31, lifting July outlays relative to ordinary July months. Elevated tariff/customs receipts partly offset that, but May 2026 context suggests refunds and tariff refunds can also absorb some gross receipts.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Prior/update/interval: prior model is recent-July first-print persistence using the 2022-2024 official MTS sample, centered at 225.2 billion. I add about 45 billion for nominal benefit, health, and interest growth versus the 2024 July outlay base, add about 55 billion for August-1 payment acceleration into July 2026, and subtract about 15 billion for elevated customs receipts net of refund risk, giving about 310 billion. The 80% interval starts from realized July dispersion of roughly 211 to 244 billion, then widens strongly for the calendar-payment shock, tariff/refund uncertainty, and missing June 2026 official print before this run.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Counter-consideration: the downside case is that payment acceleration is smaller than expected and tariff/customs collections remain high, pushing the print toward the 2024-like low 200s. The upside case is that August-payment acceleration is large, refunds are heavy, or interest/health outlays jump, pushing the deficit above 400 billion. Outside the interval would likely require either an unusually clean low-refund/high-receipt month below 170 billion or a very large payment/refund timing shock above 470 billion.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"math\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Calculation: recent-July prior average = (211.052 + 220.782 + 243.741) / 3 = 225.2 billion. Updates: +45 for nominal outlay growth, +55 for August 1 payment acceleration into July, -15 for customs/tariff receipt offset, equals 310.2, rounded to 310. Interval: 310 - 140 = 170 and 310 + 160 = 470, asymmetric to allow a fatter high-deficit payment-timing tail.\\\"},{\\\"kind\\\":\\\"forecast\\\",\\\"point\\\":310,\\\"ciLow\\\":170,\\\"ciHigh\\\":470}]}\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t19-16-44z-treasury-mts-monthly-deficit-2026-07/normalized_cells.json:3:  \\\"slug\\\": \\\"us-mts-deficit-july-2026\\\",\\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t19-31-35z-statcan-gdp-by-industry-monthly-growth-2026-05/codex_st