fferent noise: household-survey employment, labor-force entry, and rounding to one decimal place. This target resolves on 2026-06-05 under a first-print rule, with an expected ~1 week lag. The same series can also spawn next release, +3 months, +12 months, threshold questions.\\\",\\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2198289:            \\\"Tool call: bls.lookup({ survey: \\\\\\\"CPS\\\\\\\", series: \\\\\\\"unemployment_rate_sa\\\\\\\", months: [\\\\\\\"2026-01\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"2026-04\\\\\\\"], vintage: \\\\\\\"first_print\\\\\\\" })\\\"\\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2198299:            \\\"The unemployment rate pairs with payrolls but has different noise: household-survey employment, labor-force entry, and rounding to one decimal place. This target resolves on 2026-06-05 under a first-print rule, with an expected ~1 week lag. The same series can also spawn next release, +3 months, +12 months, threshold questions.\\\"\\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2198308:            \\\"The unemployment rate has held at 4.3% for two consecutive months after 4.4% in February. Claims and insured unemployment do not yet point to a sharp break, so the run keeps the center unchanged with a two-tenths 80% interval.\\\",\\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2198325:            \\\"The unemployment rate pairs with payrolls but has different noise: household-survey employment, labor-force entry, and rounding to one decimal place. This target resolves on 2026-06-05 under a first-print rule, with an expected ~1 week lag. The same series can also spawn next release, +3 months, +12 months, threshold questions.\\\"\\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2198334:            \\\"The unemployment rate has held at 4.3% for two consecutive months after 4.4% in February. Claims and insured unemployment do not yet point to a sharp break, so the run keeps the center unchanged with a two-tenths 80% interval.\\\",\\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2199178:            \\\"Average hourly earnings connects labor-market tightness to household income and price pressure, and resolves on the same schedule as payrolls and unemployment. This target resolves on 2026-06-05 under a first-print rule, with an expected ~1 day lag. The same series can also spawn next release, +3 months, threshold questions.\\\",\\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2199189:            \\\"Average hourly earnings connects labor-market tightness to household income and price pressure, and resolves on the same schedule as payrolls and unemployment. This target resolves on 2026-06-05 under a first-print rule, with an expected ~1 day lag. The same series can also spawn next release, +3 months, threshold questions.\\\"\\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2199207:            \\\"Average hourly earnings connects labor-market tightness to household income and price pressure, and resolves on the same schedule as payrolls and unemployment. This target resolves on 2026-06-05 under a first-print rule, with an expected ~1 day lag. The same series can also spawn next release, +3 months, threshold questions.\\\",\\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2199440:            \\\"Level and momentum effects point in opposite directions. April's 7.618 million level is high relative to January through March, but May payrolls were solid and unemployment was unchanged, so I expect only a partial reversal of the April jump rather than a fall back to the March trough.\\\"\\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2199449:            \\\"Level and momentum effects point in opposite directions. April's 7.618 million level is high relative to January through March, but May payrolls were solid and unemployment was unchanged, so I expect only a partial reversal of the April jump rather than a fall back to the March trough.